{% raw %}
<p>When a camera moves through a scene, each visible 3D point traces a path across the image plane. The <strong>motion field</strong> is the projection of this 3D motion onto the image: it assigns a 2D velocity to every image point. This post derives the motion field equation and shows what it reveals about scene depth and camera motion.</p>

<h2>Setup</h2>

<p>Assume a rigid, static scene and a camera with translational velocity \(\mathbf{V} = [V_x, V_y, V_z]^T\) and rotational velocity \(\boldsymbol{\Omega} = [\omega_x, \omega_y, \omega_z]^T\). A scene point \(\mathbf{P} = [X, Y, Z]^T\), expressed in the camera frame, projects to the normalized image point \(\mathbf{p} = [x, y, 1]^T\):</p>

\[
x = \frac{X}{Z}, \qquad y = \frac{Y}{Z}, \qquad \mathbf{p} = \frac{1}{Z}\mathbf{P}
\]

<figure class="blog-figure blog-figure--small">
  <img src="/img/blog/optical-flow/camera-geometry.jpg" alt="Camera coordinate axes X, Y and Z with translational velocities v and rotational velocities omega, and an image plane on which a point moves with velocity p-dot" loading="lazy">
  <figcaption>Camera frame, image plane and the six components of camera motion.</figcaption>
</figure>

<h2>Derivation</h2>

<p><strong>Step 1: Motion of the point.</strong> Relative to the camera, a static point moves opposite to the camera's motion:</p>

\[
\dot{\mathbf{P}} = -\boldsymbol{\Omega} \times \mathbf{P} - \mathbf{V}
\]

<p>Expanding the cross product gives</p>

\[
\begin{aligned}
\dot{X} &= -V_x - \omega_y Z + \omega_z Y \\
\dot{Y} &= -V_y - \omega_z X + \omega_x Z \\
\dot{Z} &= -V_z - \omega_x Y + \omega_y X
\end{aligned}
\]

<p><strong>Step 2: Differentiate the projection.</strong> Applying the quotient rule to \(\mathbf{p} = \mathbf{P}/Z\) gives \(\dot{\mathbf{p}} = \dot{\mathbf{P}}/Z - (\dot{Z}/Z)\,\mathbf{p}\), or in components:</p>

\[
\dot{x} = \frac{\dot{X} - x\dot{Z}}{Z}, \qquad \dot{y} = \frac{\dot{Y} - y\dot{Z}}{Z}
\]

<p><strong>Step 3: Substitute.</strong> Inserting the expressions from Step 1 and replacing \(X/Z\) with \(x\) and \(Y/Z\) with \(y\) gives</p>

\[
\begin{aligned}
\dot{x} &= \frac{xV_z - V_x}{Z} + xy\,\omega_x - (1 + x^2)\,\omega_y + y\,\omega_z \\[6pt]
\dot{y} &= \frac{yV_z - V_y}{Z} + (1 + y^2)\,\omega_x - xy\,\omega_y - x\,\omega_z
\end{aligned}
\]

<p>In matrix form, this is the <strong>motion field equation</strong>:</p>

\[
\dot{\mathbf{p}} =
\underbrace{\frac{1}{Z}
\begin{bmatrix}
xV_z - V_x \\
yV_z - V_y
\end{bmatrix}}_{\text{translational flow}}
+
\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}
xy & -(1 + x^2) & y \\
(1 + y^2) & -xy & -x
\end{bmatrix}
\boldsymbol{\Omega}}_{\text{rotational flow}}
\]

<h2>Translation and Rotation</h2>

<p>The two components behave very differently:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Translational flow depends on depth.</strong> It scales with \(1/Z\), so nearby points move faster in the image. This effect is known as motion parallax.</li>
  <li><strong>Rotational flow is independent of depth.</strong> \(Z\) does not appear in this term, so a purely rotating camera provides no information about scene structure.</li>
  <li><strong>Scale is ambiguous.</strong> Translation and depth appear only as the ratio \(\mathbf{V}/Z\), so a single camera cannot recover absolute scale from image motion alone.</li>
</ul>

