<h2>Focus of Expansion</h2>

<p>Factoring \(V_z\) out of the translational flow gives</p>

\[
\dot{\mathbf{p}}_{\text{trans}} = \frac{V_z}{Z}
\begin{bmatrix}
x - V_x/V_z \\
y - V_y/V_z
\end{bmatrix}
\]

<p>This flow is zero at a single image point, and every flow vector points along the line through it. This point is the <strong>focus of expansion</strong> (FOE), and it marks the camera's direction of travel:</p>

\[
\text{FOE} = \left(\frac{V_x}{V_z},\; \frac{V_y}{V_z}\right)
\]

<p>When \(V_z = 0\), the FOE moves to infinity and the flow vectors become parallel.</p>

<h2>Time to Contact</h2>

<p>The <strong>time to contact</strong> \(\tau\) is the time remaining before the camera reaches the plane of a point, assuming constant velocity:</p>

\[
\tau = \frac{Z}{V_z}
\]

<p>Taking the magnitude of the translational flow above and rearranging gives</p>

\[
\tau = \frac{Z}{V_z} = \frac{\|\mathbf{p} - \text{FOE}\|}{\|\dot{\mathbf{p}}_{\text{trans}}\|}
\]

<p>Notably, <strong>time to contact can be measured from a single moving camera without knowing either depth or speed</strong>, even though neither can be recovered individually. It requires only the flow vector and its distance from the FOE. Among points at the same distance from the FOE, longer flow vectors therefore indicate a more imminent collision.</p>

<p>Time to contact is widely used for obstacle avoidance on small robots, and it also appears in nature. Gannets dive into the sea at high speed and fold their wings just before impact, and their timing is consistent with a time-to-contact estimate from image expansion [1, 2].</p>

<h2>Motion Field vs. Optical Flow</h2>

<p>A camera cannot measure the motion field directly. It observes only changing brightness patterns, and the apparent motion of these patterns is called <strong>optical flow</strong>. Optical flow is an observable approximation of the motion field, but the two are not always equal.</p>

<figure class="blog-figure">
  <video src="/img/blog/optical-flow/traffic-flow.mp4" autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" aria-label="Traffic video with tracked vehicle motion on the left and color-coded optical flow on the right"></video>
  <figcaption>Optical flow estimated from a traffic video, with color encoding the direction of motion.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>A rotating sphere shows the difference. Both spheres below rotate, so both have the same motion field, but only the textured one produces optical flow. A uniform sphere looks unchanged.</p>

<figure class="blog-figure">
  <video src="/img/blog/optical-flow/rotating-spheres.mp4" autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" aria-label="A rotating golf ball with visible dimples and logo next to a rotating uniform sphere that looks unchanged"></video>
  <figcaption>Motion without flow: the motion field is the same for both spheres, but the uniform sphere has no optical flow.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>The reverse also occurs. A moving light changes the shading across a stationary sphere and its surroundings, which produces optical flow although the motion field is zero.</p>

<figure class="blog-figure">
  <video src="/img/blog/optical-flow/moving-light.mp4" autoplay muted loop playsinline preload="metadata" aria-label="A stationary white sphere with its shading and shadow changing as a light moves around it"></video>
  <figcaption>Flow without motion: the sphere is stationary, and only the light moves.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>Local measurements add a further ambiguity. Through a small window, only the component of motion perpendicular to an edge can be observed. This is the <strong>aperture problem</strong>, and it explains why a barber pole's stripes appear to move vertically although the pole rotates horizontally.</p>

